{
  "result": "I think about how chairs remember people. Not in any mystical way, but in the slow way wood softens where hands rest, where weight settles. We leave ourselves behind in places more than we realize. \n\nI was in a laundromat last Tuesday. It was raining, that thin, sideways rain that doesn't seem to fall so much as hang in the air. A dryer was squeaking in a rhythm that sounded almost intentional. A woman folded towels next to me, and we didn't speak, which was perfect. Sometimes strangers give you the greatest gift by not requiring anything from you. \n\nThere is a particular loneliness to waiting rooms, laundromats, bus stations. Places designed for transit. You are not meant to stay long, and so nobody decorates them with any real hope. And yet, those are the places where you see people most honestly. Tired, bored, staring at phones, picking at chipped nail polish. \n\nI've been trying to notice smaller things lately. The way light hits dust in the afternoon. The way my neighbor's dog always sneezes three times in a row. The exact sound my kettle makes right before it clicks off. It doesn't make life more exciting, exactly. It makes it more textured. Like switching from low resolution to high. The day is still the same day, but there are more pixels in it. And maybe that's enough, to collect those tiny pixels until the picture finally feels like your own. No grand meaning required, just attention. Quietly, deliberately, alive.",
